<header><h1>Rules-as-Written &amp; Community Consensus Rulings (unofficial): Notes</h1></header>
<article>
<p>Every note on this site that <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Un6F7oDY-b5oUx2RIbGH7644HJvb9jF1wtM399FuFts/edit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rules-as-Written &amp; Community Consensus Rulings (unofficial)</a> supports, with the text it is quoted for, grouped by page. <b>Explicit</b> sources state the note directly. <b>Implicit</b> sources support it without stating it.</p>
<h2><a href="/R_abilities">Abilities</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Whenever a game effect uses or grants a unit ability, treat it as if a unit were using or having that unit ability.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Metali Void Armaments: Can abilities such as the Argent commander or Jol-Nar commander affect the ANTI-FIGHTER BARRAGE roll &hellip;? A: Yes, whenever unit abilities are used, the game treats it as if a unit were using that ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_agenda_phase">Agenda Phase</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>(Thunder&rsquo;s Edge) A space station cannot be elected, although its influence can be used to cast votes.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can you vote using the influence from Trade stations? A: Yes, Space Stations are used for voting but may not be elected.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may cast votes with space stations, <i>The Triad</i>, and the <a href="/F_deepwrought">Deepwrought</a> player&rsquo;s ocean cards.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Space Stations: &ldquo;Space Stations are used for voting but may not be elected.&rdquo; Relics: &ldquo;Q: Can you vote using The Triad relic? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Deepwrought player&rsquo;s ocean cards, space stations and <i>The Triad</i> are also readied at the end of the agenda phase.<sup>3,6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Deepwrought Scholarate: &ldquo;What do Ocean Cards do? A: They are planet cards and can be spent as such.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_capture">Capture</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player who captures their own units may return them to their reinforcements at any time. They cannot place their own captured units unless instructed to do so by a game effect.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;A player cannot place (or produce) a unit captured by another player. Can a player place their own captured unit without being explicitly instructed to do so? A: No, but a player can capture their own units and can decide to return them to reinforcements at any time.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_coexistence">Coexistence</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If multiple players may choose to coexist on a planet during the same timing window, the active player chooses first; if they decline, the other player may then choose to coexist.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What if a planet owned by DWS gets invaded by the Firmament mechs? &hellip; A: Both coexistences happen in the same timing window, so it will go by active player order, the active player is given the opportunity to coexist first, and if they decline, then the other player may choose to coexist.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If only one player has units on a planet that was in coexistence, and that player was coexisting, they gain control of that planet.<sup>6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;control is exchanged immediately after the coexisting player becomes the only player with units on the planet&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Outside of the <b>Invasion</b> step of a tactical action, this happens immediately, such as when the controlling player moves all of their units off that planet.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;During the Invasion step of a tactical action, control is not exchanged until the Establish Control step. At all other times, control is exchanged immediately after the coexisting player becomes the only player with units on the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During an invasion, if the last of the controlling player&rsquo;s units on a planet are destroyed, such as by <sc>Bombardment</sc>, control does not pass to the coexisting player until the <b>Establish Control</b> step. The invading player may land ground forces and gain control of the planet, and the coexisting player remains in coexistence.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;During the Invasion step of a tactical action, control is not exchanged until the Establish Control step&rdquo; Does not state that the coexisting player remains.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who enters coexistence on a planet they control when ground forces are committed to it may still use <sc>Space Cannon</sc> against those ground forces during the <b>Space Cannon Defense</b> step. They do not lose control of the planet until the <b>Establish Control</b> step, and if they are then the only player with units on the planet, they are no longer coexisting and retain control.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;You may fire PDS defense against the active player&rsquo;s committed ground forces after entering coexistence &hellip; you do not lose control of the planet when you enter coexistence until the Establish Control step.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If multiple players are coexisting on a planet and the player that controls that planet no longer has units on it, control passes to the next coexisting player in the order used to resolve abilities in the current phase: initiative order during the action phase, otherwise clockwise order.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Control resolves to the player next in initiative order (starting with the Active Player) in the Action Phase and in Speaker order in all other phases.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All coexisting units are blockaded, regardless of what ships, if any, are in the system. This includes units that are not structures, such as mechs with the <sc>Production</sc> ability.<sup>2,6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;coexisting units are always blockaded.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Are coexisting non-structure units with PRODUCTION blockaded? A: Yes, coexisting units are always blockaded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A unit with <sc>Planetary Shield</sc> on a planet protects all units on that planet, including those of the controlling player and coexisting players. All PDS on a planet, including coexisting PDS, may use <sc>Space Cannon</sc> during the <b>Space Cannon Defense</b> step against ground forces committed by the active player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Planetary Shield from any units on the planet applies to the planet itself and thus protects all units on the planet. All PDS on a planet may use Space Cannon during Space Cannon Defense against units committed from space by the active player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player who controls a planet may start a ground combat against a coexisting player during the invasion step of a tactical action.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;if you own the planet, or win ground combat against the planet&rsquo;s owner you can attack other players who were already in coexistence at the start of this turn&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player ends their coexistence by starting a ground combat, all of their units on that planet, including structures, stop coexisting, even if they do not win the ground combat.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;When you end coexistence, you end coexistence for all of your units on that planet, including structures.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When scoring an objective, a coexisting player considers both themselves and the controlling player to control that planet. The player that controls the planet does not count a coexisting player as also controlling it.<sup>2,6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Yes, coexist&rsquo;s control counts for any rule regarding &lsquo;scoring objectives&rsquo;.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Control via coexistence is only seen by the player coexisting, so the other players see 3 planets controlled&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may score public objectives even if they coexist on, but do not control, each planet in their home system.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can a player score public objectives if they coexist in their own home system? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player must control a planet, not merely coexist on it, to explore it.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;You cannot explore a planet owned by another player&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_deploy">Deploy</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Using a <sc>Deploy</sc> ability is always optional, even if the ability&rsquo;s text does not include the word &ldquo;may&rdquo;.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Is the Iconoclast deploy ability mandatory (there is no &lsquo;may&rsquo; in the wording)? A: Deploy being a unit ability is always a may.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_elimination">Elimination</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>An eliminated player&rsquo;s home system remains a home system for game effects.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;What happens to Avernus if Muaat is eliminated?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Avernus, its token, planet card and legendary ability remain in play. Similar to an eliminated faction&rsquo;s home system.&rdquo; An eliminated player&rsquo;s home system stays a home system.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_entropic_scars">Entropic Scars</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Wormhole tokens that would be placed in an entropic scar are discarded instead.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Wormhole Generator will exhaust and place a token, which will then be discarded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Creuss player may use the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Wormhole%20Generator"><i>Wormhole Generator</i></a> technology to place or move one of their wormhole tokens into an entropic scar; that token is discarded.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Creuss use Wormhole Generator when an entropic scar is present to remove a wormhole token from the game board? A: Yes, Wormhole Generator will exhaust and place a token, which will then be discarded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The delta wormhole of the <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Flagship:~:text=Hil%20Colish"><i>Hil Colish</i></a> is not affected by an entropic scar, as the <i>Hil Colish</i> is a ship, not a wormhole token.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens to the Hil Colish delta wormhole in the entropic scar? A: The Hil Colish is placed in the Scar, not a wormhole, there no effect from Entropic Scar on the Delta wormhole opened by Hil Colish.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an exploration card would place a gamma wormhole token in an entropic scar, the card is still purged as part of its resolution, and the token is discarded.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If they would place a gamma wormhole in an entropic scar, are these cards still purged? A: The exploration card is still purged as part of its resolution. The token though is discarded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <i>Ion Storm</i> exploration card would place its token in an entropic scar, the card is still placed in the common play area, and the token is discarded.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Similar question for the Ion Storm exploration card? A: The Ion Storm card is placed in common area. The ion storm token is discarded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has ships in two (or more) entropic scars, they may spend two command tokens from their strategy pool at the start of the status phase to gain both of their faction&ndash;specific technologies.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If you have ships in both Entropic Scar anomalies at the start of the Status phase, can you opt to gain both your faction techs? A: Yes, each Entropic Scar&rsquo;s ability can be resolved as different components.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_exhausted">Exhausted</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>This includes the Deepwrought player&rsquo;s <a href="/F_deepwrought">ocean cards</a>, space stations and <i>The Triad</i>.<sup>3,6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Deepwrought Scholarate: &ldquo;What do Ocean Cards do? A: They are planet cards and can be spent as such.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_expedition">Expeditions</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The player who claimed the sixth and final slice flips the expedition token to the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge side, and places it in a system on the game board. They must choose a system that does not contain a planet, a supernova, or a printed wormhole, and is not in The Fracture, should it be in play.<sup>2,6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Place Thunder&rsquo;s Edge into a system with a printed wormhole following the other restrictions.&rdquo; The question lists the restrictions, including The Fracture.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If there is no system that meets all of these requirements, Thunder&rsquo;s Edge is placed in a system with a printed wormhole that meets the other requirements. If this is still impossible, it may be placed in a supernova.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens in the rare situation that, when Thunder&rsquo;s Edge is due to be placed, every system &hellip;? A: Place Thunder&rsquo;s Edge into a system with a printed wormhole following the other restrictions. If this is still impossible it may be placed in a supernova.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Thunder&rsquo;s Edge may be placed in a system that contains a planet destroyed by the <i>Stellar Converter</i> relic, provided that system contains no other planets (and meets the other requirements).<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Thunder&rsquo;s Edge be placed in the system that contains the remains of a Stellar Converted planet? A: Yes, so long as it contains no other planets it meets the requirements.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_exploration">Exploration</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><i>Ion Storm</i> is placed into play, not into the frontier discard pile.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Similar question for the Ion Storm exploration card? A: The Ion Storm card is placed in common area. The ion storm token is discarded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_fleet_pool">Fleet Pool</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ships may be moved into or produced in a system even if doing so would exceed the fleet pool limit. However, some of those ships must immediately be removed to satisfy the limit.<sup>2,5,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, on Auto-Factories: &ldquo;with zero in fleet you can produce 3 ships, gain the fleet token, then remove 2 ships to fleet pool.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_fracture">The Fracture</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>While The Fracture is not in play, its systems, such as the one containing Styx, are not legal targets for abilities such as those of the <a href="/F_yin">Yin</a> player&rsquo;s and <a href="/F_lizix">L1Z1X</a> player&rsquo;s heroes.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can the Yin or L1Z1X hero be used on Styx if the Fracture isn&rsquo;t in Play? A: No. The Fracture is not in play and not a legal target.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If The Fracture enters play as a result of the expedition that also places <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Thunder&#x27;s_Edge">Thunder&rsquo;s Edge</a>, Thunder&rsquo;s Edge is placed first, and then the final ingress token is placed in its system.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Thunder&rsquo;s Edge is placed, then you place the final Ingress in the Thunder&rsquo;s Edge system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Attachments, such as the <i>Nano&ndash;Forge</i> relic and the <a href="/F_ul">Ul</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Titans_of_Ul#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Terraform"><i>Terraform</i></a> promissory note, may be attached to planets in The Fracture.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;Can Nano-Forge and/or Terraform be attached to planets in The Fracture?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Can Nano-Forge and/or Terraform be attached to planets in The Fracture? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Frontier tokens are not placed in systems in The Fracture when it is brought into play, as frontier tokens are only placed during setup. However, if The Fracture is in play when the <a href="/F_empyrean">Empyrean</a> player resolves their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Empyrean#Leaders:~:text=Multiverse%20Shift"><i>Multiverse Shift</i></a> hero ability, frontier tokens will be placed in the systems in The Fracture that do not contain a planet.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Are frontier tokens placed in The Fracture when it enters play? What about when the Empyrean hero is purged? A: No, Frontier tokens are placed during setup and the fracture is not in play at this time. The Empyrean Hero will place frontier tokens into the empty systems of the Fracture if it is in play.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Al%27raith%20Ix%20Ianovar"><i>Al&rsquo;raith Ix Ianovar</i></a> breakthrough brings The Fracture into play, ingress tokens are placed this way, as if The Fracture had been rolled for, even though no roll is made.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Since the Fracture enters play without a roll via Al&rsquo;raith Ix Ianovar, do you place 6 ingress tokens, or just 4? A: Cabal BT will place Fracture as if it were rolled for.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system cannot contain two or more ingress tokens.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;the rules state that a system may not contain more than 1 ingress token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_frontier_tokens">Frontier Tokens</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Frontier tokens are not placed in systems in The Fracture when it is brought into play, as frontier tokens are only placed during setup. However, if The Fracture is in play when the <a href="/F_empyrean">Empyrean</a> player resolves their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Empyrean#Leaders:~:text=Multiverse%20Shift"><i>Multiverse Shift</i></a> hero ability, frontier tokens will be placed in the systems in The Fracture that do not contain a planet.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Are frontier tokens placed in The Fracture when it enters play? What about when the Empyrean hero is purged? A: No, Frontier tokens are placed during setup and the fracture is not in play at this time. The Empyrean Hero will place frontier tokens into the empty systems of the Fracture if it is in play.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_gravity_rift">Gravity Rift</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>(Thunder&rsquo;s Edge) A gravity rift can affect the same ship multiple times during a single movement, but it contributes its movement bonus to that ship only once.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;If a player moves a ship through the same gravity rift multiple times in one action, do they still have to roll the dice multiple times?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, although the movement bonus is once per ship per tactical action, the negative roll requirement remains per time a ship moves out of or through a gravity rift.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a ship moves through or out of multiple gravity rifts, each gravity rift provides +1 to its move value and requires a separate roll for removal. If it moves out of the same gravity rift more than once, that gravity rift provides +1 only once, but each instance requires a separate roll.<sup>1,2,6,7,8</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;If a player moves a ship through the same gravity rift multiple times in one action, do they still have to roll the dice multiple times?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, although the movement bonus is once per ship per tactical action, the negative roll requirement remains per time a ship moves out of or through a gravity rift.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_imperial">Imperial (Strategy Card)</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player coexisting on Mecatol Rex does not control it for the primary ability of <i>Imperial</i>, and so does not gain a victory point; coexisting only counts as control for scoring objectives.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If a player is Coexisting on Mecatol Rex, can they score Imperial Point with Imperial Primary? A: No, Coexistence counts as control for scoring objectives only, this does not include Imperial Point.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_legendary_planets">Legendary Planets</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If an effect instructs a player to &ldquo;end your turn&rdquo;, that player may still resolve any &ldquo;at the end of your turn&rdquo; abilities, including those of legendary planets.<sup>4,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;A previous ruling has stated that if an effect ends a player&rsquo;s turn, they may still use &lsquo;at the end of your turn&rsquo; abilities.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_neutral_units">Neutral Units</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When a hit is assigned against a group of neutral units, it is assigned to the unit that is lowest on the neutral unit reference card.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;prioritizing units lower on the reference card first.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Neutral units with <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> use it to cancel hits before any remaining hits are assigned. For example, if a neutral destroyer and a neutral dreadnought take one hit, the dreadnought uses <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> to cancel it.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Sustain window occurs before assign hits and all units with SUSTAIN will use this to cancel hits before then assigning hits to neutral units.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>During combat, hits against neutral units are always assigned in the order on the neutral unit reference card, so the speaker does not choose. The speaker does choose which neutral units are hit outside of combat, such as by <sc>Space Cannon</sc>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;How are hits assigned to neutral units outside of combat (for example, SPACE CANNON)? A: Speaker decides any decisions for the neutral units that are not hits during combat.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If there are no neutral units of a given type left in the supply, and one would be placed, it is not placed.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens if there are no more neutral units of a given type available in the supply, and another is required to be placed? A: They are not placed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_readied">Readied</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>This includes the Deepwrought player&rsquo;s <a href="/F_deepwrought">ocean cards</a>, space stations and <i>The Triad</i>.<sup>3,6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Deepwrought Scholarate: &ldquo;What do Ocean Cards do? A: They are planet cards and can be spent as such.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_space_stations">Space Stations</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player gains control of a space station when they are the only player with ships in that space station&rsquo;s system.<sup>2,6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does a player gain control of a space station before combat against neutral units, as they will be the only player with units there?&rsquo; Answer: &lsquo;Yes, there is no Neutral Player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player does not lose control of a space station if a neutral ship is placed in its system, as there is no neutral player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does a player lose control of a space station if a neutral ship is placed in its system? A: No, there is no Neutral Player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player whose ships are in a space station&rsquo;s system with only neutral units gains control of that space station before resolving combat against those neutral units, as there is no neutral player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does a player gain control of a space station before combat against neutral units &hellip;? A: Yes, there is no Neutral Player.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player can exhaust space stations to cast votes. However, space stations are not planets, so they cannot be elected.<sup>6,7,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can you vote using the influence from Trade stations? A: Yes, Space Stations are used for voting but may not be elected.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Space stations are not planets, so attachments such as the <i>Nano&ndash;Forge</i> relic and the <a href="/F_ul">Ul</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Titans_of_Ul#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Terraform"><i>Terraform</i></a> promissory note cannot be attached to them.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Nano-Forge and/or Terraform be attached to a space station? A: No, Space Stations are not planets and can&rsquo;t have attachments.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/R_status_phase">Status Phase</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>A player coexisting on, but not controlling, planets in their home system may still score public objectives.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can a player score public objectives if they coexist in their own home system? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s home system is purged by <i>The Silver Flame</i> relic, they may still score public objectives.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;Silver Flame: Is Saar affected by the &lsquo;can&rsquo;t score publics&rsquo; clause?&rdquo;: &ldquo;No, Dane has indicated he will make an exception for Saar.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_arborec">The Arborec</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><i>Stymie</i> cannot place a command token in the home system of an eliminated player.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;What happens to Avernus if Muaat is eliminated?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Avernus, its token, planet card and legendary ability remain in play. Similar to an eliminated faction&rsquo;s home system.&rdquo; An eliminated player&rsquo;s home system stays a home system.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_argent">The Argent Flight</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Argent player performs a tactical action as normal to trigger <i>Wing Transfer</i>. They cannot move ships with <i>Wing Transfer</i> into a board state that will not be resolved, such as a system that contains other players&rsquo; ships.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Why does the Argent breakthrough, Wing Transfer, have &lsquo;this action&rsquo; in its wording? &hellip; A: You still perform a tactical action as normal to trigger Wing Transfer. You may not move ships into illegal board states that will not be resolve (such as occupying the same space as other ships).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_letnev">The Barony of Letnev</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a ship has its movement value increased by an ability such as the <i>Gravity Drive</i> technology, then other ships will have their movement values increased to match the modified value.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can a fast ship have its movement boosted with Gravity Drive, and then have this modified value copied to a slow ship? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>All effects that modify a ship&rsquo;s move value, such as the <i>Gravity Drive</i> technology and the <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s agent, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Clan_of_Saar#Leaders:~:text=Captain%20Mendosa">Captain Mendosa</a>, are applied before <i>Gravleash Maneuvers</i>, and so are included in the copied value. The <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=Crucible"><i>Crucible</i></a> promissory note is the exception.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What is the interaction between Gravleash Maneuvers and Gravity Drive\Mendosa? A: All modifiers except for crucible happen before the gravleash window and apply to its bonus movement.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The value is copied after the <i>Gravity Drive</i> technology is applied. A ship that copies another ship&rsquo;s move value cannot then apply +1 from <i>Gravity Drive</i>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;can a slow ship copy a value, then get the +1 from Gravity Drive? &hellip; A: No the copy happens after gravity drive.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The +1 to move value from moving out of or through a gravity rift is not included in the move value copied by <i>Gravleash Maneuvers</i>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;How about gravity rifts? A: They are not included in the Gravleash Maneuvers move bonus.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The combat bonus of <i>Gravleash Maneuvers</i> is assigned to a ship during the <b>Roll Dice</b> step of combat, and only applies to combat rolls. It does not apply to anti&ndash;fighter barrage rolls.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Is &lsquo;before you roll dice&rsquo; literal i.e. does it apply to AFB rolls? &hellip; A: This refers to the roll dice step, and assigning which ship you will apply the ability to. Only applies to combat rolls.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Letnev player rerolls their combat dice, such as with their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Barony_of_Letnev#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Munitions%20Reserves"><sc>Munitions Reserves</sc></a> faction ability, they may assign the bonus to a different ship; the original ship loses the bonus.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;if Letnev gets to reroll their combat dice (e.g. with MUNITIONS RESERVES), do they get to choose another ship for the bonus? A: Barony can assign to a new ship but the other ship will lose the bonus.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_nomad">Nomad</a> player uses <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Nomad#Leaders:~:text=The%20Thundarian"><i>The Thundarian</i></a>, the Letnev player may assign the bonus to a different ship; the original ship loses the bonus.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If the Nomad player uses The Thundarian, can the Letnev player choose a different ship? &hellip; A: Yes, Barony can assign to a&rdquo; new ship.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_saar">The Clan of Saar</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the Saar player&rsquo;s last ship in a system is destroyed, any Floating Factories in that system are blockaded and destroyed before the <a href="/F_yin">Yin</a> player&rsquo;s agent, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Yin_Brotherhood#Leaders:~:text=Brother%20Milor">Brother Milor</a>, can be resolved to place fighters.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;if Saar loses their last ship in combat but then gets fighters from the Yin agent, is their Floating Factory blockaded during this time? A: Yes, the floating factory is blockaded and would be destroyed prior to Yin agent being resolved.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Saar player chooses which unit the hits get assigned to.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Deorbit Barrage (Breakthrough): Can the Saar player assign two hits to a unit with SUSTAIN DAMAGE? A: Yes, the targeted player uses Sustain Damage when the hit is produced.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the chosen unit has the <sc>Sustain Damage</sc> ability, it may be used to cancel the hit.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Same ruling: &ldquo;the targeted player uses Sustain Damage when the hit is produced.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Saar player may assign multiple hits to the same unit.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Same ruling: &ldquo;Can the Saar player assign two hits to a unit &hellip;? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_keleres">Council Keleres</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Riders may be played when resolving <i>Executive Order</i>.<sup>2,6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can the &lsquo;when/after agenda is revealed&rsquo; effects be played on agenda revealed by the Keleres breakthrough, Executive Order? &hellip; A: Yes, the agenda is drawn and revealed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Players exhaust planets to cast votes when resolving <i>Executive Order</i>, even if the <i>Representative Government</i> law is in play, which only affects voting during the agenda phase.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Representative Government says players get one vote during the agenda phase. Would this apply to Executive Order &hellip;? A: No, Representative Government only affects voting during the Agenda Phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects from agenda outcomes that occur &ldquo;at the end of this agenda phase&rdquo; will instead resolve at the end of the next agenda phase.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;How are &lsquo;this agenda phase&rsquo; or &lsquo;next strategy phase&rsquo; abilities resolved? A: They will remain in play until their trigger timing.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Effects from agenda outcomes that occur &ldquo;at the start of the next strategy phase&rdquo; will still resolve at the start of the next strategy phase.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;They will remain in play until their trigger timing.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When resolving <i>Executive Order</i>, the <a href="/F_argent">Argent</a> player does not vote first, but still casts additional votes.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Argent does not vote first but does still cast additional votes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_rebellion">The Crimson Rebellion</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><sc>Sundered</sc> only prevents the Rebellion player&rsquo;s ships from moving through wormholes other than epsilon wormholes. Other wormholes still make systems adjacent for the Rebellion player for all other purposes, such as <sc>Space Cannon</sc>, neighbors, objectives, action cards, and the ability of the Exile.<sup>2,6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Crimson Rebellion &ldquo;cannot use wormholes&rdquo;: &ldquo;Crimson are unable to move through wormholes. All other uses are permitted including PDS, Neighbors, placing Breaches, etc.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Rebellion player may use the <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Creuss%20IFF"><i>Creuss IFF</i></a> promissory note, but their ships still cannot move through Creuss wormholes.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can they use the Creuss IFF? A: Yes, they still may not move through the Creuss wormholes though.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Rebellion player may use the Creuss player&rsquo;s agent, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Leaders:~:text=Emissary%20Taivra">Emissary Taivra</a>, and the <i>Lost Star Chart</i> action card.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can they use the Creuss agent or the Lost Star Charts action card? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Wormhole Reconstruction</i> agenda does not override <sc>Sundered</sc>. However, the systems it makes adjacent are adjacent for movement, and the Rebellion player&rsquo;s ships do not need to use the wormholes to move between them.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does Wormhole Reconstruction override SUNDERED? A: No, but those systems are adjacent for movement, they do not need to use the worm holes so functionally it overrides Sundered.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><sc>Sundered</sc> is a state&ndash;based effect, and resolves before another player would gain control of Ahk Creuxx, even if that player is the active player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Sundered is a state-based effect which would trigger prior to gain control.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player has a ship in a system containing multiple breaches at the end of the status phase, they may remove any or all of them.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If a system contains multiple breaches during the status phase, how many can be removed &hellip;? A: All active breaches can be removed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Rebellion player is eliminated, breach tokens remain in play, and active breaches remain adjacent to each other. Inactive breaches may be removed from the game board.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Is the Sever token removed &hellip; if the Crimson Rebellion player is eliminated &hellip;? What about the breach tokens? A: &hellip; Breach tokens also remain in play and active breaches remain adjacent. Inactive breaches may be removed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Rebellion player is eliminated, their home system still destroys units that are placed or moved into it, and their faction sheet remains in play.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does the Crimson home system still destroy units that are placed or moved into it after the Crimson player is eliminated. A: Yes and the Crimson faction sheet remains in play.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Sever</i> only prevents movement through the wormhole. Adjacency, <sc>Space Cannon</sc>, the <a href="/F_creuss">Creuss</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Quantum%20Entanglement"><sc>Quantum Entanglement</sc></a> and <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Ghosts_of_Creuss#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Slipstream"><sc>Slipstream</sc></a> faction abilities, the <i>Ghost Ship</i> action card and the <i>Become the Gatekeeper</i> objective are unaffected.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Sever (Promissory Note): Does the wormhole still count for other non-movement game effects? &hellip; A: Blocks movement. Adjacency, PDS, Ghost Ship, Become the Gatekeeper, SLIPSTREAM are all unaffected.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Sever</i> affects all wormholes in the system, not just the one the token is placed on.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If there are multiple wormholes in a system &hellip;, does Severing one block them all? A: All wormholes in the system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Rebellion player is eliminated, the <i>Sever</i> token and promissory note remain in play until the note expires at the end of the status phase, when they are removed from the game.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Is the Sever token removed at the end of the status phase if the Crimson Rebellion player is eliminated &hellip;? A: The Sever token and promissory note remain in place until the note removes them in the status phase.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Rebellion player cannot use <i>Subatomic Splicer</i> to produce ships while another player&rsquo;s ships are in their home system.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Subatomic Splicer (Technology): &lsquo;A player cannot produce ships in a system that contains other players&rsquo; ships&rsquo;. Does this apply to Subatomic Splicer? A: It does apply.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Rebellion player may place a breach with the ability of an Exile at the end of a combat against neutral units.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can the Crimson destroyer, Exile, place a breach at the end of a combat with neutral units? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ahk Ravin is a swap, not a move. This means units will not trigger gravity rift rolls and the ability cannot be used to swap with a <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s space dock.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Crimson agent swapping out of a gravity rift: &ldquo;Swapping is not movement, so no roll is required.&rdquo; only the gravity-rift half is supported; Saar Spacedock claim is not in this source.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ahk Siever triggers off of combats with neutral units.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does the Crimson commander, Ahk Siever, work on combats with neutral units? A: Yes, the commander triggers on any combat involving players (which is all combats).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_deepwrought">The Deepwrought Scholarate</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the Deepwrought player uses <sc>Research Team</sc> when another player commits ground forces to a planet the Deepwrought player controls, the ground combat is skipped and the invading player gains control of the planet during the <b>Establish Control</b> step. If another player was already coexisting on that planet, the invading player may instead choose to end that player&rsquo;s coexistence, resolving a ground combat against them; the winner gains control during the <b>Establish Control</b> step.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;When DWS uses research teams to coexist, this occurs during the commit ground forces step, so when you get to the ground combat step you skip it, moving straight to the Establish Control step, where you gain control. If there were another player coexisting on that planet before invasion, though, you may choose to end that player&rsquo;s coexistence.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ocean cards are spent as though they were planet cards. However, oceans are not planets, and do not count as planets for the purpose of scoring objectives, such as <i>Push Boundaries</i>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What are the Ocean cards exactly? Do they count as planets for scoring purposes (i.e. Push Boundaries)? &hellip; A: Oceans do not count as planets for scoring. They may be spent as planet cards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When the Deepwrought player scores objectives, planets they are coexisting on do count as planets they control. This is a function of coexistence, not of oceans, and other players do not count those planets as controlled by the Deepwrought player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If DWS is coexisting on 3 planets and has 3 they control, they count as 6 when scoring Push Boundaries. How many do they control when other players want to score Push Boundaries? A: Control via coexistence is only seen by the player coexisting, so the other players see 3 planets controlled.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If all of the Deepwrought player&rsquo;s ocean cards are already in play, they cannot resolve <sc>Oceanbound</sc> to ready one of their oceans.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Oceanbound (Ability): If the Deepwrought player has all their oceans out, can they still resolve Oceanbound &hellip; so as to ready one of their oceans? A: No&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ocean cards are readied at the end of the agenda phase, like planets.<sup>3,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Deepwrought Scholarate: &ldquo;What do Ocean Cards do? A: They are planet cards and can be spent as such.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Attachments such as the <i>Nano&ndash;Forge</i> relic and the <a href="/F_ul">Ul</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Titans_of_Ul#Faction_Promissory_Note:~:text=Terraform"><i>Terraform</i></a> promissory note cannot be attached to an ocean card.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Nano-Forge and/or Terraform be attached to an Ocean Card? A: No, they are oceans&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Deepwrought player is eliminated, <i>Share Knowledge</i> and its technology remain in play until the status phase, when the promissory note is returned to the game box.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens to a technology gained via the Deepwrought Scholarate PN, Share Knowledge, if the DWS player is eliminated? &hellip; A: The DWS PN remains in play with the technology until status at which point the PN is returned to the box.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>D.W.S. Luminous</i> gains +1 for each system it moves through that contains the Deepwrought player&rsquo;s units, regardless of any other units present.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;D.W.S. Luminous (Flagship): Does it gain +1 for only your units or does it require both &hellip;? A: It gains +1 for each system if it moves through that contains your units, regardless of any other units present.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <a href="/F_argent">Argent</a> player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Argent_Flight#Faction_Technologies:~:text=Aerie%20Hololattice"><i>Aerie Hololattice</i></a> technology prevents the <i>D.W.S. Luminous</i> from moving through systems containing the Argent player&rsquo;s structures.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;D.W.S. Luminous (Flagship): How does this interact with Aerie Hololattice? &hellip; A: Aerie Hololattice stops it moving through.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Doctor Carrina may be used even if no prerequisite is skipped, but the coexisting infantry is not placed. This includes when the player chooses not to ignore any prerequisites, or researches a technology with no prerequisites. The player may ignore prerequisites they already fulfill on a technology that has at least one prerequisite.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;The agent may be used, but the coexisting infantry is not placed if a prerequisite was not skipped &hellip; The receiving player may still ignore prerequisites even if they already fulfill them on a technology that has at least 1 prerequisite.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Deepwrought player cannot use Doctor Carrina to start coexisting with the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player, as the Nekro player cannot skip a prerequisite.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Is it possible &hellip; for the Deepwrought player to start coexisting with the Nekro player by using Doctor Carrina? A: No, there is no way for Nekro to fulfill the &lsquo;they do skip a prerequisite&rsquo;&rdquo; condition.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_muaat">The Embers of Muaat</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Avernus token is purged, not returned to the Muaat player. This also purges the Avernus planet card, <i>The Nucleus</i> legendary planet ability card, and any attachments on Avernus.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens if Nova Seed is used on a system that contains the Avernus token? A: The token is purged, this causes the planet card, legendary ability card, and any attachments to be purged as well.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Nova Seed</i> cannot target the home system of an eliminated player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;What happens to Avernus if Muaat is eliminated?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Avernus, its token, planet card and legendary ability remain in play. Similar to an eliminated faction&rsquo;s home system.&rdquo; An eliminated player&rsquo;s home system stays a home system.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Nova Seed</i> cannot replace a system tile in the Fracture, such as the system containing Styx.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can the Muaat Hero, Nova Seed, be used on tiles in the Fracture? A: No, you cannot nova seed tiles which contain multiple systems.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Muaat player may move Avernus even if another player controls it.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Stellar Genesis: &ldquo;If another player has units on Avernus, and Muaat moves the planet into a supernova, what happens to those units? A: They are unaffected.&rdquo; The question assumes the Muaat player moves Avernus while another player has units on it.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Muaat player moves Avernus into a supernova while another player has units on it, those units are unaffected.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Stellar Genesis (Breakthrough): If another player has units on Avernus, and Muaat moves the planet into a supernova, what happens to those units? A: They are unaffected.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Avernus is not a ship. It does not roll for removal when it moves out of or through a gravity rift.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Avernus and gravity rifts: &ldquo;Avernus does not roll for gravity rift, it is not a ship.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Avernus may be moved out of a system that contains the Muaat player&rsquo;s command token during a tactical action, even if there are ground forces on it, as it is not a ship.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Avernus be moved out of a locked system during a tactical action with Ground Forces on Avernus? A: Yes, it is not a ship.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Muaat player is eliminated, Avernus, its token, its planet card, and its legendary planet ability card remain in play.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does the Avernus remain in play if the Muaat player is eliminated? A: Avernus, its token, planet card and legendary ability remain in play.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_hacan">The Emirates of Hacan</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the planet traded contains coexisting units, those units remain coexisting. Because the mech is moved after control of the planet is exchanged, the player gaining the planet may use their <i>Integrated Economy</i> technology, or a similar ability, before coexistence would end.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Coexisting units remain in a state of coexistence as ownership changes through Hacan mech &hellip; The Hacan mech specifically moves units *after* exchanging control and so allows the gaining player to use Integrated Economy before&rdquo; coexistence ends.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The new fleet pool token may be used to support the ships just produced.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Auto-Factories (Breakthrough): Can the new fleet token be used to support the newly placed ship? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The produced ships do not need to remain to gain the fleet pool token. For example, a Hacan player with no command tokens in their fleet pool may produce three ships, gain the command token, then remove two of those ships due to their fleet pool.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Do the ships have to survive fleet pool limits to trigger the new token? A: No, with zero in fleet you can produce 3 ships, gain the fleet token, then remove 2 ships to fleet pool.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_empyrean">The Empyrean</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a system is separated from an anomaly by the Empyrean player&rsquo;s <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Empyrean#Breakthrough:~:text=Void%20Tether">Void Tether</a> token, <i>Aetherstream</i> can only be used for that system if the Empyrean player allows it. This includes the <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player using a copy of <i>Aetherstream</i>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If one side is an anomaly, and another player activates the other side, can Aetherstream be used (especially if Nekro is copying it)? A: Only if Empyrean allows it.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system that contains a planet destroyed by the <i>Stellar Converter</i> relic, and no other planets, is considered to contain no planets.<sup>4,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, on Thunder&rsquo;s Edge: &ldquo;so long as it contains no other planets it meets the requirements.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Empyrean player moves a ship into a system containing one of the <a href="/F_saar">Saar</a> player&rsquo;s Floating Factories, and no other ships, that <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Clan_of_Saar#Space_Dock:~:text=Floating%20Factory">Floating Factory</a> will be blockaded and destroyed before the Empyrean player can unlock Xuange. If the Empyrean and Saar players are not neighbors in any other way, then Xuange will not unlock.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Also, if Saar loses their last ship in combat but then gets fighters from the Yin agent, is their Floating Factory blockaded during this time? A: Yes, the floating factory is blockaded and would be destroyed prior to Yin agent being resolved.&rdquo; Blockade destruction happens immediately, before other abilities resolve.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A system that contains a planet destroyed by the <i>Stellar Converter</i> relic, and no other planets, is considered to contain no planets.<sup>4,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, on Thunder&rsquo;s Edge: &ldquo;so long as it contains no other planets it meets the requirements.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If The Fracture is in play when the Empyrean player resolves <i>Multiverse Shift</i>, frontier tokens will be placed in the systems in The Fracture that do not contain a planet.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Are frontier tokens placed in The Fracture when it enters play? What about when the Empyrean hero is purged? A: &hellip; The Empyrean Hero will place frontier tokens into the empty systems of the Fracture if it is in play.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Other players treat the systems that share the border with the Void Tether token as not being adjacent for all game effects, even if both systems contain matching wormholes. The Empyrean player continues to treat them as adjacent.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Void Tether (Breakthrough): If the Void Tether token is between two systems that both contain a wormhole, do other players consider those systems adjacent? A: No, RAW the systems are not adjacent via any means.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Empyrean player has units or controls a planet in one of the systems, they will be neighbors with any player with units or controlling a planet in the other, and vice versa.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does the QUANTUM ENTANGLEMENT neighbors ruling apply i.e. if Empyrean is on one side of the Void Tether token, are they neighbors &hellip;? A: Yes, they must be neighbors as Empyrean cannot block themselves with void tether.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Empyrean player is eliminated, <i>Void Tether</i> remains in play, and the Void Tether tokens continue to function.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Do the void tether tokens remain in play if the Empyrean player is eliminated? A: The Empyrean Breakthrough remains in play and the Void Tethers remain functional.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_sol">The Federation of Sol</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Ships produced with <i>Bellum Gloriosum</i> increase the number of fighters and ground forces that may be produced, even if those ships must then be removed to satisfy the fleet pool limit.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does the ship have to survive to generate the additional PRODUCTION? Could a Sol player with zero fleet pool just build useless carriers solely to boost their infantry PRODUCTION? A: Given When timing yes, they can produce a carrier and additional 6 infantry and then remove the carrier to fleet pool.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_firmament">o The Firmament / x The Obsidian</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Firmament_/_The_Obsidian#Faction_Specific_Components:~:text=Enervate"><i>Enervate</i></a> plot may be resolved whenever the Obsidian player resolves the secondary ability of a strategy card in the puppeted player&rsquo;s play area, including via the <i>Strategize</i> action card.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does the Enervate plot card work on any secondary usage &hellip;? A: The puppeted player must have the strategy card in their play area, if you then play Strategize or otherwise resolve the secondary you may resolve Enervate.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player is eliminated, their control tokens are removed from any plot cards. If a plot card then has no control tokens on it, that plot card returns to the Firmament player&rsquo;s hand.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens to a plot card if the player whose command token on it is eliminated? A: Remove that players token and draw it if it has no players&rsquo; tokens on it.&rdquo;</li>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If there are no other player tokens on the Plot, it returns to hand.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Firmament player has their control token on a secret objective that is elected by the <i>Classified Document Leaks</i> agenda, they do not gain a victory point, and they cannot score it again.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens if a secret objective is elected as a public objective, but the Firmament has control token on it? Do they immediately get VP? Can they score it again for VP? A: They do not gain a VP and they are unable to score it again.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Purging the Firmament player&rsquo;s home planet cards does not allow them to score the <i>Become a Martyr</i> objective.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Puppets of the Blade and Become a Martyr: &ldquo;do they qualify for BecomeA Martyr? A: No.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Firmament player may choose not to place a plot. If they do not, the rest of <i>Black Ops</i> still resolves, including purging it.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;The Firmament player may choose not to play a plot. The rest of the note, including purging it, still resolves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Malevolency</i> remains in the receiving player&rsquo;s hand, not their play area. However, it is mandatory during the status phase; this is an exception to all other promissory notes.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Malevolency (Obsidian Promissory Note): Does this go into the receiver&rsquo;s play area? &hellip; A: It remains in a player&rsquo;s hand but is mandatory during status, this is an explicit exception to all other promissory notes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Obsidian player cannot move an ingress token into a system that already contains an ingress token.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can the Vuil&rsquo;raith player move an ingress token into a system that already contains a different ingress token? A: No, the rules state that a system may not contain more than 1 ingress token.&rdquo; Stated for the Vuil&rsquo;raith; the same rule applies to Planesplitter.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Obsidian player cannot move an ingress token into a system in The Fracture.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, on the Vuil&rsquo;raith breakthrough: &ldquo;Can the Vuil&rsquo;raith player move an ingress token into the Fracture &hellip;? A: No, ingress tokens cannot be placed in the Fracture.&rdquo; The answer is stated generally, so it covers Planesplitter too.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Planesplitter</i> resolves before the <i>Coup D&rsquo;etat</i> action card can be played and resolved.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What is the interaction between Coup D&rsquo;etat and the Obsidian&rsquo;s Planesplitter technology? A: Planesplitter would resolve before Coup D&rsquo;etat is played and resolved.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Space cannon cannot be used against any of the moving ships. However, only ships that are not transporting units may move through systems containing other players&rsquo; ships.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;No space cannon against any ships moving. Only ships not transporting may move through other player&rsquo;s ships.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Firmament player may score the <i>Stake Your Claim</i> objective using Captain Aroz with ships in a single&ndash;planet system where the planet is controlled by another player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can you score Stake Your Claim via commander with ships in a single planet system controlled by another player? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If Captain Aroz causes the Firmament player to treat a planet as controlled by them, the Firmament player may use it only to score secret objectives, while the planet&rsquo;s actual controller may still use it to score any objective.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If CaptainAroz causes the Firmament player to treat a planet as controlled by them, does that stop the actual controller from scoring a secret objective with it? A: No, both are treated as controlling it for purposes of scoring objectives.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Firmament player may use this ability while they are the active player, to commit the mech and start coexisting.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can you use Firmament&rsquo;s mech when you are the active player (to commit the Mech &amp; start coexisting)? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The trade goods from <i>The Reaping</i> are gained as two separate gains. The <a href="/F_mentak">Mentak</a> player may use their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Mentak_Coalition#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Pillage"><sc>Pillage</sc></a> faction ability for each.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does receiving trade goods from the Reaping count as 2 separate gains (for the purposes of Pillage)? A: Two separate gains.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_creuss">The Ghosts of Creuss</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <i>Creuss IFF</i> may place wormhole tokens in The Fracture.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Wormhole Generator (Technology): Can wormholes be placed in The Fracture? Also applies to placing wormholes with Creuss IFF. A: Yes, wormholes can be placed in the Fracture.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Creuss wormhole tokens may be placed in The Fracture.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Wormhole Generator (Technology): Can wormholes be placed in The Fracture? Also applies to placing wormholes with Creuss IFF. A: Yes, wormholes can be placed in the Fracture.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Neutral ships prevent a Creuss wormhole token from being placed in their system, in the same way as another player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;Do neutral ships block wormhole placement?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Do neutral ships block wormhole placement? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Creuss player may use <i>Wormhole Generator</i> to place a wormhole token in an entropic scar; the card is exhausted and the token is then discarded.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Creuss use Wormhole Generator when an entropic scar is present to remove a wormhole token &hellip;? A: Yes, Wormhole Generator will exhaust and place a token, which will then be discarded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Creuss wormhole tokens may be placed in The Fracture.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Wormhole Generator (Technology): Can wormholes be placed in The Fracture? Also applies to placing wormholes with Creuss IFF. A: Yes, wormholes can be placed in the Fracture.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Neutral ships prevent a Creuss wormhole token from being placed in their system, in the same way as another player&rsquo;s ships.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;Do neutral ships block wormhole placement?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Do neutral ships block wormhole placement? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Creuss player may use <i>Wormhole Generator &Omega;</i> to place a wormhole token in an entropic scar; the card is exhausted and the token is then discarded.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Creuss use Wormhole Generator when an entropic scar is present to remove a wormhole token &hellip;? A: Yes, Wormhole Generator will exhaust and place a token, which will then be discarded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Hil Colish</i> is not a wormhole token. If it is in an entropic scar, its delta wormhole is unaffected by that entropic scar.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens to the Hil Colish delta wormhole in the entropic scar? A: &hellip; there no effect from Entropic Scar on the Delta wormhole opened by Hil Colish.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Each wormhole in the system provides the <sc>Production</sc> value and discount, even those of the same type.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Do multiple wormholes of the same type count separately for the Creuss breakthrough, Particle Synthesis? &hellip; A: Yes, each wormhole, even wormholes of the same type,&rdquo; provides it.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_lizix">The L1Z1X Mindnet</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>When the L1Z1X player gains control of a planet, they also replace coexisting structures on that planet.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Do the L1Z1X replace coexisting structures when they gain control of a planet? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_bastion">Last Bastion</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the Bastion player gains control of an uncontrolled planet, they may choose if they resolve <sc>Liberate</sc> before or after they explore that planet.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Bastion must choose which order to resolve Liberate and Exploration per planet, then resolve those abilities in full before the other.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If they explore first, and the number of infantry or the resource value of the planet changes while resolving the exploration, then the new values will be used when resolving <sc>Liberate</sc>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Same ruling: each ability is resolved &ldquo;in full before the other,&rdquo; so the later one sees the changed values.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Galvanize tokens are placed from the Bastion player&rsquo;s reinforcements. If there are none in their reinforcements, a galvanize token cannot be moved from elsewhere on the board.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can a Galvanize token be moved when there is none left in the reinforcements? &hellip; A: No, Galvanise tokens are placed from reinforcements. Unlike Sleepers there is no move or place option.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The additional die rolled by a galvanized unit is mandatory.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Galvanize: Is the extra dice mandatory? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Abilities referring to the Bastion player&rsquo;s galvanized units being &ldquo;present&rdquo; count only galvanized units that are participating in the combat.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What does &lsquo;each of your galvanized units present&rsquo; mean? &hellip; A: Present == participating.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Bastion player is eliminated, galvanize tokens remain on other players&rsquo; units, and those units continue to roll one additional die. The Bastion player&rsquo;s faction sheet remains in play until there are no galvanize tokens on the game board.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does Galvanize still work when the Last Bastion player is eliminated from the game? A: Yes, the Galvanized Tokens remain attached to non-Bastion units and they continue the roll one additional die for unit abilities and combat rolls (the Bastion faction sheet remains in play until there are no more Galvanize tokens on the game board).&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>X&ndash;89 Bacterial Weapon &Omega;&Omega;</i> technology doubles hits before abilities that cancel hits, such as <i>Proxima Targeting VI</i>, are resolved. The planet is still exhausted if the Bastion player controls it.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;X89 turns all hits into 2 before abilities and effects like Titan&rsquo;s agent or Proxima. You will exhaust the planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Abilities that affect bombardment rolls, such as the <i>Plasma Scoring</i> technology, the <a href="/F_argent">Argent</a> player&rsquo;s commander, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Argent_Flight#Leaders:~:text=Trrakan%20Aun%20Zulok">Trrakan Aun Zulok</a>, or the <a href="/F_jol_nar">Jol&ndash;Nar</a> player&rsquo;s commander, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Universities_of_Jol-Nar#Leaders:~:text=Agnlan%20Oln">Agnlan Oln</a>, may be applied to the <sc>Bombardment</sc> roll from <i>Proxima Targeting VI</i>.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Proxima Targeting VI (Technology): Can the Bastion player apply abilities such as Plasma Scoring/Argent commander or Jol-Nar commander to the BOMBARDMENT roll? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_mahact">The Mahact Gene&ndash;Sorcerers</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li><i>Scepter of Dominion</i> cannot place command tokens in the home system of an eliminated player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;What happens to Avernus if Muaat is eliminated?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Avernus, its token, planet card and legendary ability remain in play. Similar to an eliminated faction&rsquo;s home system.&rdquo; An eliminated player&rsquo;s home system stays a home system.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The active player may still use any &ldquo;at the end of your turn&rdquo; abilities.<sup>4,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;A previous ruling has stated that if an effect ends a player&rsquo;s turn, they may still use &lsquo;at the end of your turn&rsquo; abilities.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_mentak">The Mentak Coalition</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <a href="/F_nekro">Nekro</a> player cannot place a Valefar token on the Corsair card.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Corsair (Breakthrough Unit): Can Nekro copy Corsair from Mentak? &hellip; A: They cannot copy corsair.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Mentak player is still considered to own Cruiser II while it is under Corsair. As such, the Nekro player may copy Cruiser II.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Same ruling: &ldquo;mentak is still considered to own cruiser II.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_naaz_rokha">The Naaz&ndash;Rokha Alliance</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Naaz&ndash;Rokha mechs are only Z&ndash;Grav Eidolon during the <b>Space Combat</b> step, only in the active system, and only in the space area.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;Z-Grav Eidolon (Mech): Is flipping to a Z-Grav Eidolon a (mandatory) activated ability &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Mech Flips at start of Space Combat in player order timing.&rdquo; The mechs become Z-Grav Eidolon only once space combat starts.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The mech flips at the start of space combat, resolved in the normal player order for abilities at that timing.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Z-Grav Eidolon (Mech): Is flipping to a Z-Grav Eidolon a (mandatory) activated ability &hellip;? A: Mech Flips at start of Space Combat in player order timing.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Naaz&ndash;Rokha player owns the <i>Assault Cannon</i> technology, they may count their Z&ndash;Grav Eidolon toward the three non&ndash;fighter ships, once they resolve its ability. However, if another player then resolves an ability that reduces the number of non&ndash;fighter ships the Naaz&ndash;Rokha player has in the active system to less than three, the Naaz&ndash;Rokha player will no longer be able to resolve the <i>Assault Cannon</i> technology ability.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;Z-Grav Eidolon (Mech): Is flipping to a Z-Grav Eidolon a (mandatory) activated ability &hellip;?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Mech Flips at start of Space Combat in player order timing.&rdquo; The question concerns Assault Cannon: the flip is an ability resolved in player order at the start of space combat, so the attacker may resolve first.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Eidolon Maximum is considered a ship for all relevant game effects.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Eidelon Maximum treated as a ship: &ldquo;Yes, it is a ship in that system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Eidolon Maximum participates in space combat even when it is on a planet.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Eidelon Maximum (Breakthrough Unit): Does the Eidelon Maximum participate in space combat when it is on a planet? A: Yes, it is a ship in that system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When on a planet, the Eidolon Maximum blocks other players&rsquo; ships from moving through the system, counts toward objectives that require ships, and can blockade space docks.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;In general, is the Eidelon Maximum treated as a ship when on a planet? Does it block other player&rsquo;s ship movement through the system? Can it count towards ship objectives? Can it blockade space docks? A: Yes, it is a ship in that system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Additional Eidolon Maximums may be placed while the <i>Articles of War</i> law is in play.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can additional Eidelons Maximums be placed when the Articles of War law is in play? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_nekro">The Nekro Virus</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the Nekro player has a Valefar Assimilator Z token on the <a href="/F_vuilraith">Vuil&rsquo;raith</a> player&rsquo;s faction sheet, copying their flagship, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Vuil&#x27;Raith_Cabal#:~:text=The%20Terror%20Between"><i>The Terror Between</i></a>, they will capture destroyed units. They may also capture their own units, and may return their own captured units to their reinforcements at any time.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What is the interaction when Nekro has Vuil&rsquo;raith&rsquo;s flagship &hellip;? A: Nekro can capture their own units and can decide to return them to reinforcements at any time.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Nekro player copies the <a href="/F_firmament">Firmament</a> player&rsquo;s flagship, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Firmament_/_The_Obsidian#Flagship:~:text=Heaven%27s%20Eye"><i>Heaven&rsquo;s Eye</i></a>, references to &ldquo;your plots&rdquo; refer to the Firmament player&rsquo;s plots.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If the Nekro Virus copies the Firmament flagship, can they somehow utilize its ability (it says &lsquo;1 of your plots&rsquo;)? A: Nekro references the Firmaments plots when copying the Firmament flagship.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Nekro player has copied the Firmament player&rsquo;s flagship, and the Firmament becomes the Obsidian, the Valefar Assimilator Z token is moved to the Obsidian player&rsquo;s flagship.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens with the Z ability on the Firmament flagship when they become Obsidian? A: The Z token is moved to the Obsidian Flagship.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a player is eliminated while the Nekro player has a Valefar Assimilator Z token on their faction sheet, the Nekro player keeps the copied flagship abilities; that faction sheet remains in play for this purpose.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does the Nekro Virus keep the faction flagship abilities of eliminated players? A: Yes, this follows other rulings on components that stay in play after a player is eliminated, those player&rsquo;s faction sheets remain in play for this ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_ral_nel">The Ral Nel Consortium</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If the Ral Nel player&rsquo;s ships leave a system or are destroyed, their structures in the space area remain there until they are blockaded, transported, or placed onto planets.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens to Ral Nel structures in space if their ships leave the system/are destroyed? A: They remain in space until they are blockaded, transported, or placed onto planets.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Linkships cannot fire <sc>Space Cannon</sc> against adjacent units, even if the Ral Nel player has PDS II.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Linkship (Unit): If the Ral Nel player has the PDS II unit upgrade, can their Linkships&rsquo; SPACE CANNON be used to target ships in adjacent systems? A: No, Link Ship 1 only allows use of Space Canon unit ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a space dock gains <sc>Space Cannon</sc> from the <i>Experimental Battlestation</i> action card, each Linkship II in the system may use that <sc>Space Cannon</sc> ability.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What is the interaction between Experimental Battlestation and Linkship II? &hellip; A: Yes, previous rulings on EBS have clarified that the dock gains the ability during the movement step, thus it can be used by Linkship&rsquo;s ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If this ability destroys the last of the other player&rsquo;s ships in the system, the Ral Nel player&rsquo;s ships still retreat and the ability resolves.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens if the flagship ability destroys the last player&rsquo;s ship in the system? Do you still retreat? A: Yes, the ships retreat and the ability resolves.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The ability of Kan Kip Rel resolves fully before the action card hand limit is checked.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Kan Kip Rel (Agent): How would this resolve if the Ral Nel player would go over their action card hand limit? A: The ability resolves before checking hand limit size.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the <a href="/F_yssaril">Yssaril</a> player uses this ability, they draw three action cards instead of two due to their <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Yssaril_Tribes#Faction_Abilities:~:text=Scheming"><sc>Scheming</sc></a> faction ability. They discard one card from those drawn or from their hand, then take one of the drawn cards and give another to another player, keeping the third if it was not discarded.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Draw 3 instead of 2, discard 1 from the 3 drawn or hand to scheming, then take 1 and give the other to another player keeping the 3rd if not discarded to scheming.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Watchful Ojz is a &ldquo;when&rdquo; ability. It resolves before the <i>Intercept</i> action card is played and resolved.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Watchful Ojz (Commander): How does Watchful Ojz interact with the Intercept action card &hellip;? A: Commander is a When, resolve Watchful Ojz, then Intercept is played and resolved.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Purging Director Nel does not prevent the active player from scoring the <i>Prove Endurance</i> objective. The active player scores <i>Prove Endurance</i> when they pass, then the Ral Nel player purges their hero.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Director Nel (Hero): Does purging Director Nel block another player from scoring Prove Endurance? A: No, the active player can score Prove Endurance when they pass. RalNel then purges their hero.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ground forces may only be moved from adjacent systems, not from within the active system.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can their mech move the infantry within the system &hellip;? A: No, only from adjacent systems, not the active system.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If an Alarum moves another Alarum into the system, the newly moved Alarum cannot use its ability to move additional ground forces.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If an Alarum pulls in another Alarum, can that new Alarum immediately pull in additional ground forces? A: No, it does not see the trigger.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Action cards on <i>Data Skimmer</i> are not in the discard pile.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What happens to the action cards stored on Ral Nel breakthrough, Data Skimmer, if the Ral Nel player is eliminated? A: &hellip; all action cards on it are immediately discarded.&rdquo; They are discarded only then.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the Ral Nel player is eliminated, <i>Data Skimmer</i> is returned to the game box, and all action cards on it are immediately discarded.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;The breakthrough is returned to the box and all action cards on it are immediately discarded.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_norr">Sardakk N&rsquo;orr</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Planets in the home system of an eliminated player will not contribute to unlocking G&rsquo;hom Sek&rsquo;kus.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;What happens to Avernus if Muaat is eliminated?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Avernus, its token, planet card and legendary ability remain in play. Similar to an eliminated faction&rsquo;s home system.&rdquo; An eliminated player&rsquo;s home system stays a home system.</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_ul">The Titans of Ul</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If <i>Terraform</i> is attached to a planet in The Fracture, that planet may be explored.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;If Terraform is attached, can planets in The Fracture be explored? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li><i>Terraform</i> may be attached to <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/Thunder&#x27;s_Edge">Thunder&rsquo;s Edge</a>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Terraform be attached to Thunder&rsquo;s Edge? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Ul player may use the <sc>Deploy</sc> ability of a Hecatoncheires to place units into coexistence, most often through the use of <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Titans_of_Ul#Breakthrough:~:text=Slumberstate%20Computing"><i>Slumberstate Computing</i></a>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Titans use the Mech&rsquo;s deploy ability for coexistence? A: Yes.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A planet controlled by another player may contain at most one sleeper token.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Slumberstate Computing, limit of Sleeper tokens on another player&rsquo;s planet: &ldquo;Is there a limit to the number of Sleeper tokens that may be placed on another player&rsquo;s planet? A: One per planet&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When another player agrees to allow the Ul player to place a sleeper token, the agreement includes which planet the token will be placed on.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does the agreement to place a Sleeper token require agreeing to a specific planet &hellip;? A: Agreement includes which planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The additional action cards are only drawn during the <b>Draw Action Cards</b> step of the status phase. Other effects that draw action cards during the status phase, such as the <i>Minister of Policy</i> agenda or the <a href="/F_mentak">Mentak</a> player&rsquo;s agent, <a href="https://twilight-imperium.fandom.com/wiki/The_Mentak_Coalition#Leaders:~:text=Suffi%20An">Suffi An</a>, do not draw additional action cards.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Does that mean that you&rsquo;ll be receiving additional AC for the Minister of Policy &hellip;? A: No, the ability is specifically Status Phase Step 3 Draw Action Cards.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_jol_nar">The Universities of Jol&ndash;Nar</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>Due to the synergy of <i>Specialized Compounds</i>, the Jol&ndash;Nar player may exhaust a planet with a yellow technology specialty to research a green technology, and vice versa.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Jol-Nar use the SYNERGY to exhaust a yellow skip planet to pay for researching a green technology, or vice versa? A: Yes&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The <i>Psychoarchaeology</i> technology cannot be used to avoid exhausting the planet with the technology specialty.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Jol-Nar use Psychoarchaeology to bypass exhausting the technology specialty when using Specialised Compounds? A: No&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_vuilraith">The Vuil&rsquo;raith Cabal</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>If a ship moves through or out of the same gravity rift multiple times, that gravity rift provides +1 to its move value only once, but the ship rolls for removal each time.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;If a player moves a ship through the same gravity rift multiple times in one action, do they still have to roll the dice multiple times?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Yes, although the movement bonus is once per ship per tactical action, the negative roll requirement remains per time a ship moves out of or through a gravity rift.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Vuil&rsquo;raith player cannot choose to roll for The Fracture when they gain <i>Al&rsquo;raith Ix Ianovar</i>; the breakthrough&rsquo;s ability replaces the roll.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Al&rsquo;raith Ix Ianovar (Breakthrough): Can the Vuil&rsquo;raith player choose to perform a breakthrough roll first? A: No, the breakthrough replaces the roll with its own ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When The Fracture is brought into play by <i>Al&rsquo;raith Ix Ianovar</i>, the Vuil&rsquo;raith player places ingress tokens according to the synergy on the Cabal breakthrough.<sup>6,9</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Since the Fracture enters play without a roll via Al&rsquo;raith Ix Ianovar, do you place 6 ingress tokens, or just 4? A: Cabal BT will place Fracture as if it were rolled for.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>They cannot move an ingress token into a system that already contains an ingress token.<sup>6,7</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can the Vuil&rsquo;raith player move an ingress token into a system that already contains a different ingress token? A: No, the rules state that a system may not contain more than 1 ingress token.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>Ingress tokens cannot be placed in The Fracture. The Vuil&rsquo;raith player cannot move an ingress token into a system in The Fracture, even if they have a space dock in that system.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can the Vuil&rsquo;raith player move an ingress token into the Fracture by gaining their breakthrough after the Fracture is in play &hellip;? A: No, ingress tokens cannot be placed in the Fracture.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_xxcha">The Xxcha Kingdom</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The Xxcha player cannot gain control of a space station with <sc>Peace Accords</sc>, as a space station is not a planet.<sup>2,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Peace Accords (Ability): Can Xxcha gain control of a space station with PEACE ACCORDS? A: No, it is not a planet.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The active player may still use any &ldquo;at the end of your turn&rdquo; abilities.<sup>4,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;A previous ruling has stated that if an effect ends a player&rsquo;s turn, they may still use &lsquo;at the end of your turn&rsquo; abilities.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_yin">The Yin Brotherhood</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The <a href="/F_firmament">Firmament</a> and the Obsidian each have their own alliance card.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;When the Obsidian isn&rsquo;t being played, and a game effect instructs a player to gain their alliance ability &hellip;? A: Each has their own alliance card.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/F_yssaril">The Yssaril Tribes</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The discarded action card may be any card in the Yssaril player&rsquo;s hand; it does not have to be one of the cards they just drew.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Draw 3 instead of 2, discard 1 from the 3 drawn or hand to scheming.&rdquo; The discard may come from the hand.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The Yssaril player may allow another player to use one of their faction abilities whenever that ability would be used. That player may use it for the duration of that ability.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;What is the timing window for allowing other players to use your faction abilities with the Yssaril breakthrough, Deepgloom Executable? What is the duration of this? A: Whenever that ability would be used, for the duration of that ability.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
</ul>
<h2><a href="/C_action_cards">Action Cards</a></h2>
<ul>
    <li>The active player may still use any &ldquo;at the end of your turn&rdquo; abilities.<sup>4,6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;A previous ruling has stated that if an effect ends a player&rsquo;s turn, they may still use &lsquo;at the end of your turn&rsquo; abilities.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player may target themselves with <i>Crisis</i>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Q: Can you play Crisis on yourself? A: Yes, you are a valid target for Crisis.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The agreement between the players includes the planets on which the infantry will be placed.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Q: Exchange Program: Does each player propose where the infantry will be placed &hellip;? A: The agreement includes the planets where infantry will be placed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If <i>Coup D&rsquo;etat</i> cancels the strategic action the affected player performs, that player has not yet performed their next action. <i>Extreme Duress</i> remains in play until that player&rsquo;s next turn, when they must still resolve a strategic action or suffer its effect.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Coup D&rsquo;etat cancels the action, so the active player has still not performed their &lsquo;next action.&rsquo;&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The destroyer cannot be placed in the home system of an eliminated player.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, &ldquo;What happens to Avernus if Muaat is eliminated?&rdquo;: &ldquo;Avernus, its token, planet card and legendary ability remain in play. Similar to an eliminated faction&rsquo;s home system.&rdquo; An eliminated player&rsquo;s home system stays a home system.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If players make a deal for one of them to play an action card, <i>Lie In Wait</i> resolves after that action card is played and resolved.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Q: Lie In Wait: Say two players make a deal for one of them to play an action card. &hellip; A: Lie in Wait would resolve after that action card is played and resolved.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>A player cannot target a planet they control with <i>Mercenary Contract</i>.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Can Mercenary Contract be played on your own planet? A: No, you cannot target harmful effects on your own planets, unless explicitly allowed.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a planet card is returned to the planet card deck, the player that previously controlled it will no longer do so. They remove their control token from the planet.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Mercenary Contract: &ldquo;If the planet becomes neutral, does it get re-explored &hellip; when a player invades later? A: Yes, the planet card will be retrieved from the planet card deck&rdquo;. The planet becomes neutral, so no player controls it.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the planet card is returned to the planet card deck, then when a player later gains control of that planet, they will explore it.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Q: Mercenary Contract: If the planet becomes neutral, does it get re-explored &hellip;? A: Yes, the planet card will be retrieved from the planet card deck and it will trigger both exploration and relic depending on its traits.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>The planet will not be explored if it does not have a trait. If the planet is in The Fracture, however, the gaining player will draw a relic.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;it will trigger both exploration and relic depending on its traits.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If a legendary planet card is returned to the planet card deck, the player that controlled it also loses the associated legendary planet ability card.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Implicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus, Mercenary Contract: &ldquo;What happens regarding the exhausted/readied status of a legendary planet ability card when a legendary planet is returned and then recontrolled? A: It is returned from the deck readied.&rdquo; The ability card goes back with the planet card.</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>When a player later gains control of that planet, they gain the legendary planet ability card readied.<sup>6</sup>
        <ul>
        <li><b>Explicit</b>: <sup>6</sup> Community consensus: &ldquo;Mercenary Contract: What happens regarding the exhausted/readied status of a legendary planet ability card when a legendary planet is returned and then recontrolled? A: It is returned from the deck readied.&rdquo;</li>
        </ul></li>
    <li>If the planet card is returned to the planet card deck, all cards attached to that planet are purged.<sup>6</sup>
